    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 4 (Questions 19&ndash;24)</div>\n<p>In 1944, Trinidadian historian Eric Williams published <i>Capitalism and Slavery</i>, a watershed work that fundamentally reconfigured the historiography of the Atlantic world. Williams advanced two bold, intertwined theses that shattered contemporary orthodoxies. First, he argued that the vast profits generated by the triangular transatlantic trade and Caribbean chattel slave plantations provided the indispensable capital accumulation that financed Britain's Industrial Revolution. In Williams's formulation, it was the blood, sweat, and uncompensated labor of millions of enslaved Africans that directly underwrote the rise of British industrial supremacy. Second, Williams contended that the eventual abolition of the British slave trade in 1807 and the emancipation of enslaved laborers in 1833 were not the glorious moral triumphs of high-minded Christian humanitarians like William Wilberforce, as imperial historiography had long boasted. Rather, Williams argued that slavery was dismantled because it had become an economically unviable, obsolete impediment to industrial capitalism. Once British manufacturers realized that free trade and domestic wage labor yielded higher profits than protected West Indian sugar monopolies, they abandoned the Caribbean planter class.</p>\n<p>For decades, British imperial historians fiercely resisted the &ldquo;Williams thesis,&rdquo; seeking to restore the primacy of evangelical philanthropy and moral idealism in abolitionist history. The most potent empirical challenge came from economic historian Seymour Drescher in his 1977 monograph <i>Econocide: British Slavery in the Era of Abolition</i>. Through meticulous statistical analysis of trade volumes, prices, and plantation returns, Drescher demonstrated that the British Caribbean sugar economy was not declining on the eve of abolition. On the contrary, British slave plantations were enjoying an era of robust profitability and commercial expansion between 1780 and 1807. By terminating a booming, highly profitable imperial trade, Drescher argued, the British Parliament was committing calculated &ldquo;econocide&rdquo;&mdash;an economic sacrifice driven primarily by popular political mobilization, humanitarian ideology, and evangelical agitation rather than structural economic obsolescence.</p>\n<p>In recent decades, however, a sophisticated new economic historiography&mdash;represented by scholars such as Joseph Inikori, Robin Blackburn, and the &ldquo;New History of Capitalism&rdquo; school&mdash;has vindicated the core insight of Williams's first thesis while refining its mechanical premises. Early critics of Williams often pointed out that profits directly remitted from plantation agriculture accounted for only a modest percentage of total British national income. But modern scholars emphasize that the significance of the transatlantic slave economy cannot be captured by simple accounting balance sheets. The slave trade functioned as the institutional linchpin of globalized capitalism. It stimulated massive backward and forward industrial linkages: British metallurgical manufacturing expanded to produce slave-trading shackles, muskets, and brassware; Lancashire cotton mills grew entirely dependent on slave-grown raw cotton; and marine insurance, commercial banking, and maritime shipping infrastructure flourished specifically to service Atlantic slave commerce.</p>\n<p>Thus, while Drescher successfully demonstrated that abolition was not forced by immediate plantation insolvency, Williams's broader structural argument remains profoundly intact. British industrial capitalism did not arise in a pristine domestic laboratory of free markets and inventive genius; it emerged from an Atlantic commercial complex deeply entangled with colonial coercion and human commodification. Abolition itself did not represent a pure, disinterested triumph of moral virtue, but a complex realignment: the emergent industrial bourgeoisie could afford to discard the slave trade precisely because centuries of Atlantic coercion had already established the global commercial hegemony that made British free trade viable.</p>",
