    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>For millennia, traditional Eurocentric and Sinocentric historical narratives portrayed the pastoral nomadic peoples of the Eurasian steppe&mdash;from the Scythians and Huns to the Turks and Mongols&mdash;as destructive, uncivilized barbarians whose sole historical role was the periodic pillaging of sophisticated sedentary agrarian empires. In Chinese imperial dynastic chronicles and Greco-Roman annals alike, nomads were depicted as subhuman horse-riding raiders emerging without warning from the frozen northern wastelands to sack flourishing cities, burn libraries, and slaughter agrarian populations before retreating back into their trackless wilderness. This civilizational paradigm established a rigid, moralized dichotomy: sedentary urban civilization represented order, literature, law, and technological progress, whereas pastoral nomadism represented chaos, cruelty, and cultural stagnation.</p>\n<p>Modern global environmental historians and nomadic pastoral anthropologists have thoroughly dismantled this simplistic civilizational prejudice, revealing that pastoral nomads were not the destroyers of Eurasian civilization, but its essential circulatory system and chief architectural catalysts. The vast ecological expanse of the Eurasian steppe, stretching from the Hungarian plains to the Manchurian forests, was not a barrier separating East from West, but a dynamic transcontinental highway. Pastoral nomadism was not an underdeveloped prelude to farming, but a highly sophisticated, capital-intensive ecological specialization that extracted vital nourishment from semi-arid grasslands where sedentary rain-fed agriculture was ecologically impossible. By managing diverse herds of horses, sheep, and camels, nomadic pastoralists maximized mobility and ecological resilience across extreme seasonal temperature swings.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, pastoral nomadic confederations were the indispensable catalysts of transcontinental technological diffusion. By establishing horse-breeding stations, mountain passes, and well-watered river corridors across the Central Asian deserts, nomadic horsemen ensured the safe passage of Sogdian merchants, Buddhist pilgrims, Nestorian Christian monks, and Islamic scholars. Great oasis cosmopolitan emporiums such as Samarkand, Bukhara, and Dunhuang flourished under nomadic patronage, functioning as dynamic intellectual melting pots where Hellenistic artistic forms, Persian administrative customs, and Chinese papermaking technology cross-pollinated to generate a truly unified Eurasian cultural ecumene.</p>\n\n<p>Because pastoral nomadic confederations lived in symbiotic tension with settled empires, they required continuous access to agrarian manufactured goods, grains, silks, and metals. When peaceful trade broke down, nomads deployed their superior cavalry mobility and composite bow technology to compel sedentary rulers to reopen border trade markets or pay massive annual subsidies. Crucially, in creating trans-Eurasian empires, nomadic rulers established the institutional security and diplomatic infrastructure of the ancient and medieval Silk Roads. The famous <i>Pax Mongolica</i> of the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries united China, Central Asia, Persia, and the Russian principalities under a single administrative and postal relay network (the <i>Yam</i> system), fostering unprecedented intercontinental transmission of gunpowder, printing, astronomical knowledge, paper currency, and architectural styles across Eurasia.</p>\n<p>The historical significance of pastoral nomadic empires lies therefore in their role as trans-civilizational integrators. Rather than operating as isolated predatory parasites, nomadic confederations served as the kinetic vectors of global connectivity, violently shattering the provincial isolation of regional agrarian empires and knitting fragmented cultural zones into an interconnected world system. Long before European maritime explorers ventured across the Atlantic and Indian oceans, the pastoral nomads of the Eurasian steppe had already pioneered the first true era of continental globalization, demonstrating that the mobility of the frontier was just as foundational to world history as the monumental architecture of the sedentary metropolis.</p>",
