    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>In the mid-twentieth century, the Martinican psychiatrist and philosopher Frantz Fanon transformed anti-colonial thought by diagnosing colonialism not merely as a system of military occupation and economic expropriation, but as a profound psycho-affective pathology. In his 1952 text <i>Black Skin, White Masks</i>, Fanon utilized psychoanalytic theory to examine how the colonial apparatus inflicts an existential wound upon the colonized. Colonialism does not merely subjugate physical bodies; it constructs an epistemological hierarchy in which whiteness is equated with humanity, beauty, reason, and civilization, while blackness is constructed as lack, savagery, and bodily irrationality. Trapped in this Manichean schema, the colonized subject experiences an agonizing alienation&mdash;an internalized neurosis characterized by the desperate, impossible quest to attain white recognition by donning &ldquo;white masks&rdquo; (mastering the metropolitan language, adopting European mannerisms, and rejecting indigenous heritage). Yet because racialized colonial society refuses ontological recognition to the Black subject, this imitation leads only to profound self-hatred, psychological fragmentation, and existential paralysis.</p>\n<p>In his 1961 revolutionary manifesto <i>The Wretched of the Earth</i>, written amidst the brutal Algerian War of Independence, Fanon shifted his analytical gaze from individual neurosis to collective political liberation. Here, Fanon confronted the foundational violence of the colonial state. Colonialism, Fanon argued, is not a thinking machine; it is &ldquo;violence in its natural state, and it will only yield when confronted with greater violence.&rdquo; For Fanon, revolutionary counter-violence was not an end in itself or an outburst of mindless savagery, but an indispensable dialectical therapy. On an existential level, violence acts as a &ldquo;cleansing force&rdquo;: it frees the colonized native from their internalized inferiority complex, shatters the despairing paralysis instilled by decades of colonial subjugation, and restores their mutilated human dignity. By participating in collective armed struggle, the colonized cease to be passive spectators of history and reconstitute themselves as self-determining historical agents.</p>\n<p>Yet Fanon was far from a naive celebrant of post-colonial triumph. In the prophetic chapter &ldquo;The Pitfalls of National Consciousness,&rdquo; Fanon issued an urgent warning regarding the dangers awaiting newly liberated nations. He observed that following the formal withdrawal of colonial administrations, political power is frequently captured by the national bourgeoisie&mdash;an educated, urban elite that lacks genuine productive capacity or entrepreneurial vision. Rather than transforming economic structures or uplifting the impoverished agrarian peasantry, this comprador class merely steps into the shoes of the departed colonial masters. The national bourgeoisie becomes an intermediary agent of global neo-colonial capitalism, monopolizing state resources, indulging in vulgar self-enrichment, and maintaining social hierarchies through authoritarian state terror. National consciousness, Fanon warned, degenerates into an empty shell if it is not accompanied by a radical social revolution that redistributes land and empowers the rural masses.</p>\n<p>Contemporary scholars continue to debate Fanon's legacy, particularly his controversial defense of revolutionary violence. Liberal critics argue that Fanon underestimated the destructive cyclicality of violent insurrection, contending that militarized liberation struggles often institutionalize authoritarian cultures that plague post-colonial states long after independence. Conversely, post-colonial theorists argue that Fanon's philosophical brilliance lay in his refusal of sentimental pacifism in the face of structural brutality: he demonstrated that peace without justice is merely the silence of subjugation. Fanon remains indispensable because he insisted that authentic decolonization requires a total ontological transformation: the creation of a &ldquo;new man,&rdquo; liberated from both colonial bondage and the parasitic compromises of the post-colonial state.</p>",
