    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 8 (Questions 43&ndash;48)</div>\n<p>Between 1347 and 1351, the Black Death&mdash;a catastrophic pandemic of bubonic, pneumonic, and septicemic plague caused by the bacterium <i>Yersinia pestis</i>&mdash;swept across Eurasia and North Africa, killing an estimated thirty to sixty percent of Europe's population. In traditional historiography, this biological apocalypse was often treated as an unmitigated disaster that plunged medieval society into religious hysteria, macabre despair, and institutional paralysis. However, over the past century, social and economic historians have recognized that the demographic shock of the Black Death served as the decisive structural catalyst that dismantled the feudal mode of production, fundamentally reconfiguring class power, labor relations, and the trajectory of European capitalism.</p>\n<p>From the perspective of classical neo-Malthusian economic history, the pandemic abruptly resolved the acute demographic crisis of the late medieval world. By the early fourteenth century, Europe had experienced centuries of rapid population growth that pressed against ecological limits. Land had become severely fragmented, marginal soils were overcultivated, real wages had plunged to near-starvation levels, and aristocratic landlords extracted onerous feudal rents from a subjugated, land-hungry peasantry. When the plague suddenly eliminated between a third and a half of the labor force, it inverted the land-to-labor ratio overnight. Land, once scarce and exorbitantly expensive, suddenly lay abundant and fallow; labor, previously cheap and expendable, instantly became an extraordinarily scarce and valuable commodity.</p>\n<p>This demographic inversion precipitated an unprecedented crisis for the feudal nobility. Surpluses of grain rotted in fields as desperate landlords competed against one another for scarce agricultural laborers. To attract and retain tenants, lords were forced to slash rents, abolish customary servile obligations (such as the <i>corv&eacute;e</i>, or unpaid forced labor on the lord's demesne), and offer higher cash wages. When English lords attempted to use parliamentary legislation&mdash;most notoriously the Statute of Laborers of 1351, which made it a crime to pay or demand wages above pre-plague rates&mdash;peasants defied the regulations, migrating across parish lines to seek the highest bidder. When the crown imposed regressive poll taxes to fund aristocratic warfare, smoldering class tensions exploded in the English Peasants' Revolt of 1381. Although the revolt was brutally suppressed militarily, serfdom in Western Europe had received a mortal wound; within a century, servile bondage had effectively vanished across England and France, replaced by contractual tenancy and wage labor.</p>\n<p>Yet the social consequences of the Black Death cannot be explained by mechanical demographic models alone. In a seminal 1976 essay that ignited the celebrated &ldquo;Brenner Debate,&rdquo; Marxist historian Robert Brenner exposed a profound historical paradox: while the demographic collapse of the plague struck both Western and Eastern Europe with comparable ferocity, the institutional outcomes were diametrically opposed. In Western Europe, peasant resistance and customary village solidarity led to the erosion of serfdom and the rise of agrarian capitalism. In Eastern Europe (Poland, Prussia, and Russia), by contrast, the very same demographic crisis triggered the &ldquo;second serfdom&rdquo;: powerful aristocratic landlords crushed weak peasant communities, enserfed previously free farmers, tied laborers to the soil, and established coercive agrarian export regimes. As Brenner demonstrated, demography provides only the raw material of history; the decisive variable that determines social evolution is the balance of class power and the structural resilience of rural political institutions.</p>",
