    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 9 (Questions 49&ndash;53)</div>\n<p>In the mid-eighteenth century, the French philosopher Voltaire enacted a profound revolution in Western historical writing. Prior to Voltaire's landmark works&mdash;most notably <i>The Age of Louis XIV</i> (1751) and <i>Essai sur les m&oelig;urs et l'esprit des nations</i> (1756)&mdash;European historiography was dominated by two stifling genres. On one hand stood providential universal history, epitomized by Bishop Jacques-B&eacute;nigne Bossuet's 1681 <i>Discourse on Universal History</i>, in which human events were treated as a divine puppet show orchestrated by Christian providence to lead humanity inexorably toward Catholic salvation. On the other hand stood dry royal annals: pedantic, chronological registers compiled by court antiquarians that cataloged dynastic successions, diplomatic treaties, and bloody military skirmishes without offering a shred of sociological insight or philosophical reflection.</p>\n<p>Voltaire swept aside both genres, inaugurating modern secular, philosophical historiography. He declared that the true subject of history is not the triumphs of kings or the mysterious will of God, but <i>l'histoire de l'esprit humain</i>&mdash;the history of the human mind, customs, arts, sciences, commerce, and societal manners. In <i>The Age of Louis XIV</i>, Voltaire deliberately relegated military campaigns to a secondary status, dedicating the core of his work to the flourishing of Moli&egrave;re's theater, Racine's tragedies, the founding of scientific academies, and the commercial reforms of Colbert. Furthermore, in the <i>Essai sur les m&oelig;urs</i>, Voltaire dismantled Bossuet's Judeo-Christian parochialism. Bossuet had treated the ancient Hebrews as the gravitational center of world history, reducing vast civilizations like China, India, and the Islamic world to irrelevant footnotes. Voltaire boldly began his global history with imperial China and ancient India, celebrating their antiquity, secular administrative morality, and technological achievements long before the emergence of European kingdoms.</p>\n<p>Yet for all its revolutionary brilliance, Enlightenment historiography possessed profound philosophical blind spots that nineteenth-century historicists would vigorously expose. Voltaire's historical imagination was fundamentally constrained by his militant anti-clerical rationalism. Viewing history through a militant unilinear teleology of progress, Voltaire treated past ages not as autonomous cultural worlds with their own internal coherence, but as stages in a cosmic struggle between Enlightenment reason and clerical superstition. For Voltaire, the entire European Middle Ages were simply a thousand-year wasteland of &ldquo;barbarism, ignorance, and religious fanaticism,&rdquo; unworthy of sympathetic historical comprehension. Because he applied the universal, ahistorical standards of eighteenth-century Parisian salons to all past epochs, Voltaire failed to understand that human values, institutions, and worldviews are historical products that must be understood within their own cultural contexts.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the Enlightenment conquest of history remained double-edged. By liberating historical inquiry from theological dogma and dynastic hagiography, Voltaire established cultural and social history as legitimate disciplines, expanding the historian's canvas to encompass the global tapestry of human civilization. Yet by viewing the past through an uncritical lens of presentist moralism, he created a new secular orthodoxy that mistook contemporary European rationalism for the universal summit of human achievement. True historical understanding, as later generations of thinkers from Herder to Ranke would demonstrate, required moving beyond Voltaire's condescension toward the past, learning to appreciate each epoch not as an imperfect prelude to the present, but as a unique manifestation of the human spirit.</p>",
